course: dictionary
lesson: 5224
title: Softened words: euphemisms
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Some words are bent into softer forms so they sound less strong. These are called euphemisms. «Long ago it was thought wrong to say God's or Jesus's name in anger, so people bent the words.» Jeez is one of these softened words: it is a short, milder version of «Jesus».
explain: Look for clue words. «"Jeez" is a mild word used instead of "Jesus", said in surprise or annoyance.» The word mild tells you the word is less strong than the one it replaces. The feeling tells you what it means: surprise or annoyance. Ask yourself, what is the speaker feeling?
explain: Jeez is casual, everyday speech, so you will hear it in chat and talk between friends. It is not a polite word for every place, though. Some religious people still find it disrespectful, so think about who is listening before you use it.
fact: A euphemism is a softer or milder word used in place of a stronger one.
fact: «Jeez» is a cry of surprise, annoyance or shock.
fact: «Jeez» is a short stand-in for «Jesus», a name many people hold as holy.
fact: Other softened words follow the same pattern: «gosh» and «golly» for «God», «gee» and «jeez» for «Jesus», and «crikey» and «cripes» for «Christ».
fact: Some religious people still find «jeez» disrespectful, so it is wise to save it for casual talk and avoid it in school writing.
exercise: «"Jeez," said Mum, when she saw the mess in the kitchen.» What feeling does jeez show? | Joy at a birthday party | Sleepiness | *Surprise or annoyance | Hunger
exercise: What is a euphemism? | A word with no meaning at all | *A softer or milder word used in place of a stronger one | A word only used by children | A word that means exactly the same as a long word
exercise: Long ago people bent God's name into a softer word. Which word is one of them? | Crikey | *Gosh | Cripes | Jeez
test: «"Jeez," said Ella, "that was close!"» Which word does jeez stand in for? | Crikey | Cripes | *Jesus | Gosh
test: Why might some people think jeez is disrespectful? | It means very hungry | It is only found in old books | *It is a softened form of a name many people hold as holy | It is a long, difficult word
exam: Ella's friend says «Jeez, you scared me!» What does this tell you, and why should Ella think before she uses it? | Very happy, and it is a holiday word with no meaning | Hungry, and it is a polite word everywhere | *Surprised or shaken, and some people find it disrespectful | Sleepy, and it is only for school
